%% mad_stream_writer_boot.glp --- a writer end that travels INSIDE A STREAM
%% ELEMENT, one level below the link, and the holder's assignment coming back
%% (SGSG Code's report of 2026-09-08 12:53 UTC; IGLP madglp-spec, Definition
%% Globalize).  alice cold-calls bob with a stream, then writes
%% [hello, probe(W) | _] on it keeping W's reader; bob reads the stream and
%% assigns W; alice's value arrives.  The first step of the warm call.

procedure boot.
boot :-
    agent(ch(_?, _), ch(_?, _))@alice,
    say(alice_init, alice, [bob], ch(_?, _))@alice,
    agent(ch(_?, _), ch(_?, _))@bob,
    say(bob_init, bob, [], ch(_?, _))@bob.

%% Every agent's initial goal is agent(ch(_?,_),ch(_?,_)), the person channel first and the network channel second (GLP-Spec maglp.tex, Definition "Multiagent GLP"), which the program, mad_stream_writer/, answers: a boot defines no agent/2, and the person goal's first act names the entry the program starts (IGLP 30c04c5, Implementation Notes, "Boot").  The person scripted at each agent, say/4, run on its person channel in the person's place, tells the agent the entry, with its own key and the keys of the peers its person knows, its ground arguments; an agent is its public key, and a readable name here is a variable standing for one (Remark "Agent Names"), the agent's key having been made at its spawn.

%% The person at each agent: it tells the agent its role, its key and the keys of the peers it knows, its ground arguments, and reads nothing.
PersonSays ::= init(Constant, Key, List(Key)).

exported procedure say(Constant?, Key?, List(Key)?, Channel(Stream(Constant), Stream(PersonSays))?).
say(R, Me, Ps, ch(_, [init(R?, Me?, Ps?)])).
